+# The colours that get a column
+
+The field does not stop at its thirty-five samples. Its rule supplies colours between them, but only selected positions have been given a column. Sampling makes some values stand before the visitor while others remain underfoot. The gaps also let a body pass. A less sparse selection would change more than the number of colours on display: it would change the available room.
+
+All the columns have the same dimensions. Their colour differences acquire equal architectural weight, although the regular spacing is itself a choice. This is a particular collection of the field, not a complete inventory of its possibilities. The unshaded floor and the lit columns also give their shared computed values different visible conditions.
+
+The framed wall makes another kind of collection. Its palette titles invoke moods, artists and movements; the shipped colour lists are authored interpretations, not evidence that a history has one correct palette. Their membership comes from those lists, not from the coordinate rule on the floor. Preserve the difference between an attribution and a claim to represent everything it names.
+
+A swatch can leave that company. Its material and label travel with it. The hexadecimal RGB code records a colour value; the word beneath it is selected by proximity to a short list of named RGB values. One name can receive many distinct values. Naming makes a colour available to conversation while also gathering differences under a common word.
+
+The room lets the visitor compare these choices through movement: enter the gaps, read an address, take a swatch away from its group. None of those actions is a colour-comprehension test. The critical question is how a field becomes a collection, and how a collection gives particular colours a body, a name and neighbours.
+
